{
  "dom_id": "step-q116238",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A car is traveling at a constant speed on a circular road. The force $F,$ in newtons ($N$), needed to keep the car from skidding is inversely proportional with the radius $r,$ measured in meters, of the circular road. If the radius of the road is $400\\,m,$ a force of $1\\,200\\,N$ is required to prevent the car from skidding. What is the constant of variation?",
  "worked_solution": "The force $F$ is inversely proportional to the radius $r$ of the road, so we have\n\n$$\nF=\\frac{k}{r},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the radius is $r=400\\,\\text{m},$ the required force is $F=1200\\,\\text{N}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,200 & =\\frac{k}{400} \\\\ 1\\,200\\cdot 400 & =k \\\\ 480\\,000 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=Fr,$ we have that the units of $k$ are $\\text{N}\\cdot \\text{m}.$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k=480\\,000\\,\\text{N}\\cdot \\text{m}.$",
